+  release), 7.1 cache entries and records read back with the previous
+  release's settings, and jobs using only the envelope and serializers of
+  Rails 7.2. Pinned bytes: a session cookie (replayed: the user stays
+  signed in), a mail link, cache entries and a job (`BigDecimal` argument)
+  written by the deployed Rails 7.2.4 (`RAILS_72_*`), and the Rails 7.0.8.7
+  entries and mail link (`RAILS_70_*`). Rails 7.2.4 and 8.1.4 were compared
+  file by file: same cookie jars (except that 8.1 counts the cookie name in
+  the 4KB limit), session store, message verifiers, encryptors and codecs
+  (8.1 decodes JSON leniently), key generator, CSRF tokens, cache coder,
+  serializers and entries, record marshalling, and job envelope (8.1 only
+  adds `ActionControllerParametersSerializer`).
+* `test/unit/lib/redmine/rollback_compatibility_test.rb`: key derivation,
+  verifier and cookie rotations, the message formats, and every source and
+  precedence of `keep_secret_key_base`.
+* Full suite (sqlite3, Ruby 3.4.8, Rails 8.1.4, `load_defaults 8.1`):
+  5369 runs, 0 failures, 0 errors, 41 skips (repositories, ImageMagick and
+  LDAP not available). The only deprecation warning is Redmine's own (see
+  "What changed"); the other warnings come from gems (`ostruct`,
+  `benchmark`, rubyzip). RuboCop: no offenses.
+* Development: `rake db:migrate redmine:load_default_data
