+  description goes into the formatted text cache. Each consumer replays them:
+  still signed in, filter kept, form submitted with each token and still
+  signed in, unwatch link works, cache entries read, issue rendered from the
+  other release's formatted text cache.
+  * Every check passes for previous -> this (upgrade), this -> previous
+    (rollback), the chains previous -> this -> previous -> this and
+    this -> previous -> this -> previous, Rails 7.0 -> this (a user who
+    skipped the previous release) and Rails 7.0 -> previous -> this -> previous,
+    Rails 6.1 -> this and Rails 6.1 -> this -> previous; with
+    `SECRET_KEY_BASE` set next to `secret_token.rb`, and with the secret in
+    `config/secrets.yml` (another one in `secret_token.rb`), both ways.
+  * Negative checks, all failing as expected: without `keep_secret_key_base`,
+    both secret setups sign the user out and break the mail link (404);
+    this -> Rails 7.0 (two releases) raises on the cache entries (500);
+    Rails 6.1 -> this without `:hybrid` signs the user out.
+
+### Ops notes for this release
+
+* Deploy with Ruby 3.4.8 (unchanged) and `bundle install` (Rails 8.1.4). No
+  migrations. sqlite3 2.9.6 is now a precompiled gem for x86_64-linux
+  (glibc): no C compiler needed for it there; on other platforms add yours
+  with `bundle lock --add-platform` (or let Bundler compile it).
+* **Don't change where `secret_key_base` comes from**: `ENV['SECRET_KEY_BASE']`,
+  the credentials, `config/secrets.yml` or `config/initializers/secret_token.rb`,
+  first one set wins, as with every release since Rails 7.0. A warning is
+  logged when it comes from `config/secrets.yml`: move that same value to
+  `secret_token.rb` (or `SECRET_KEY_BASE`) when convenient.
+* Rolling back to the previous release (Rails 7.2.4, its own `Gemfile.lock`)
+  is safe: no one is signed out, forms and mail links keep working, it reads
+  the cache entries this release writes.
+* **Don't roll back two releases at once** (to Rails 7.0): it raises on the
+  cache entries this release writes (`rake tmp:cache:clear` for the file
+  store, or flushing memcache, would be required first).
+* Jobs are still enqueued immediately, even inside a transaction (Rails 8.1
+  dropped `enqueue_after_transaction_commit`). With a persistent job queue,
+  jobs enqueued by this release run on the previous release, unless a plugin
+  passes `ActionController::Parameters` to a job.
+* YJIT stays off (see the overrides). `Regexp::TimeoutError` in the logs
+  means a regexp took more than a second (see "Framework defaults adopted");
+  `Regexp.timeout = nil` in `config/additional_environment.rb` turns the
+  limit off.
+* Plugins: their Gemfiles are evaluated by Redmine's, so a dependency on
+  Rails < 8 (or roadie-rails < 3.4, sqlite3 < 2.1) breaks `bundle install`.
+  Rails 8.1 deprecates (removal in Rails 8.2 or 9.0) `ActiveSupport::Configurable`,
+  `String#mb_chars`, hash arguments to route methods and dynamic
+  `:controller`/`:action` route segments; still gone since the previous
+  release: the positional `serialize :attr, Class` form, `require` of files in
+  `app/`, raw SQL with double-quoted string literals on SQLite and
+  `ActiveSupport::Deprecation.warn` (use `Redmine::Deprecator.warn`).
+
+## Work left for the next release
+
+1. **Write only what this release reads, read what it writes.** Compare the
+   next Rails version with Rails 8.1.4 (cookie jars, session store, message
+   verifiers and encryptors, key generator, CSRF tokens, cache coder and
+   entries, record marshalling, job envelope and serializers,
+   `secret_key_base` resolution) and its new framework defaults; override any
+   new format for one release (this release can't read what doesn't exist
+   yet). If only Ruby is upgraded (Ruby 4.0), check Marshal, JSON and YAML
+   both ways like the Ruby 3.4 upgrade did.
+2. Update `test/integration/rollback_compatibility_test.rb`: this release
+   becomes the previous one. Replace the `RAILS_72_*` pins with the same
+   values written by this release (Rails 8.1.4 writes the same cookie, mail
+   link and job formats; its cache entries are in the 7.1 format, so
+   `previous_release_cache_store` and `as_previous_release` become plain 8.1
+   defaults), keep the Rails 7.0 read tests while its data can be around
+   (item 4), and move the next release's own formats to the write side.
+3. `cookies_serializer = :hybrid` and the CBC/SHA1 cookie rotations of
+   `Redmine::RollbackCompatibility` are **not** rollback overrides and must
+   not be retired blindly: a user whose session cookie dates from Rails 6.1
+   is signed out without them (verified). Redmine refreshes a session token
+   (`tokens`, `action = 'session'`) at least every minute it is used, and
+   every request since the Rails 7.0 deployment rewrote the cookie in the
+   current format. So retire them only if, with `redmine_verify_sessions` on
+   (the default), no session token was last used before the Rails 7.0
+   deployment:
+   `Token.where(:action => 'session').where('updated_on < ?', <Rails 7.0 deployment time>).none?`
+   Otherwise keep them (they cost nothing). With them gone, `:json` writes
+   the same JSON.
+4. Time-based cleanups (ops know the dates):
+   * The SHA1 verifier rotations (mail links signed by Rails 6.1) can go in a
+     release deployed at least 30 days after the previous release (Rails 7.2)
+     was deployed: Rails 6.1 could no longer run after that, and the links
+     expire after 30 days.
+   * Mail links signed by Rails 7.0 (Marshal) expire 30 days after the
+     previous release was deployed or after the last rollback to Rails 7.0.
+     Until then, keep `message_serializer` reading Marshal
+     (`:json_allow_marshal`, the current default).
+   * Cache entries written by Rails 7.0 (6.1 format) never expire. Rails 8.1
+     reads them through its Marshal fallback; if the next Rails version stops
+     reading them, clear the cache on deployment (or keep them readable).
+5. Keep `Redmine::RollbackCompatibility.keep_secret_key_base` until ops have
+   moved the secret to a single source (no "secrets.yml" warning in the logs,
+   and no `secret_token.rb` next to `SECRET_KEY_BASE` or the credentials).
+6. While Rails 8.1 is a rollback target: keep the serialized columns on
+   `coder: YAML`, and don't pass job arguments Rails 8.1 can't deserialize.
+7. Revisit the functional overrides (forgery origin check, open redirects,
+   mail queue name, preload links, YJIT, commit callbacks, callbacks order,
+   column serializer, `belongs_to` required), see their "Remove when".
+8. `ActiveRecord::Base.connection` is soft-deprecated since Rails 7.2 (no
+   warning unless `active_record.permanent_connection_checkout = :deprecated`):
+   38 calls in 16 files of `app/` and `lib/`, plus plugins. Move them to
+   `with_connection` or `lease_connection` before Rails warns about it.
+9. For the next Ruby upgrade (Ruby 4.0): add `ostruct` (net-ldap) and
+   `benchmark` (mini_magick; Rails 8.1 no longer depends on it, so Ruby 3.4
+   now warns at boot) to `Gemfile`, update rubyzip (chilled string warning),
+   and RuboCop for the new syntax (`TargetRubyVersion` is still 2.7).
+10. SQL Server (`activerecord-sqlserver-adapter ~> 8.1.0`, `tiny_tds ~> 3.0`)
+    isn't covered by CI (postgresql, mysql2 and sqlite3 are).
+11. Rails warns about tests without assertions (34, from 6 test
+    methods).
+
+## Previous release: Rails 7.2 on its framework defaults, Ruby 3.4 (deployed)
+
+* Rails 7.2.4 and Ruby 3.4.8, `config.load_defaults 7.2`, `Gemfile.next` on
+  Rails 8.1.4 (same configuration) for CI. `Gemfile.lock` was the
+  `Gemfile.next.lock` of the release before, re-resolved under Ruby 3.4.8
+  (sqlite3 1.6.9 compiled from source, Bundler 2.6.9).
+* It wrote what Rails 7.0 read, with two rollback overrides
+  (`cache_format_version = 7.0`, `marshalling_format_version = 6.1`), and
+  signed mail links on the Rails 7.1 message defaults (JSON, metadata with the
+  data), which Rails 7.0 read with `NewerMessageFormats`. It read the 7.1
+  cache and record formats natively, which let this release retire the
+  overrides.
